            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec</strong><br>\nHenri de Toulouse-Lautrec was born into an aristocratic family in the south of France. Despite his noble heritage, Lautrec faced significant challenges from a young age due to health issues. He suffered from a genetic condition that prevented his legs from developing properly, which significantly affected his height and mobility. However, these difficulties did not impede his love for art, as he began drawing and painting in his early teens, using art as a form of escape and expression.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Art Education and Influences</strong><br>\nToulouse-Lautrec moved to Paris to study art under the guidance of famous painters like Léon Bonnat and Fernand Cormon. Paris was the heart of the art world at that time, and the young artist was heavily influenced by the Impressionists and Japanese printmakers. He adopted a style that favored bold contours and flat areas of color, distinct from the finer brushwork of traditional European painting. Lautrec's experience in Paris's vibrant artistic community was integral to his development as an artist.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Montmartre and Bohemian Life</strong><br>\nMontmartre, the Parisian district known for its bohemian lifestyle, became Lautrec's home and his source of inspiration. He immersed himself in its colorful nightlife, capturing the scenes of cafes, cabarets, circuses, and brothels. The artist became a fixture of this district, mingling with the performers and patrons, often sketching their portraits and scenes of Parisian life that conveyed the liveliness and spectacle of the era.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
