            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Unique Artistic Style</strong><br>\nLautrec's unique artistic style captured the energy and character of Parisian nightlife. His work is characterized by a daring use of lines and strategic use of color that brought his subjects to life. He was not interested in idealizing his subjects but rather in portraying them with all their flaws and personality, making his art feel more real and immediate.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Contribution to Poster Art</strong><br>\nLautrec's contribution to poster art is particularly noteworthy. He is credited with elevating the status of the advertising poster to a respected art form. His posters for the Moulin Rouge and other entertainment venues were striking in design and color, which helped define the visual culture of his time. These posters were celebrated for their bold graphics and sharp wit, qualities that made them stand out on the busy streets of Paris.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Influence of Personal Struggles</strong><br>\nThe personal struggles of Toulouse-Lautrec were deeply etched into his work. His physical limitations often led him to seek refuge in his art and the liberal atmosphere of Montmartre, which accepted him despite his disabilities. Alcohol became a means of solace and social lubricant for the artist, but it also exacerbated his health issues over time, influencing the darker tones and themes in some of his later works.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
