          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Legacy in Art History</strong><br>\nDespite his early death at the age of 36, Toulouse-Lautrec left a significant legacy in art history. He produced a vast array of works, including paintings, posters, and illustrations that reflected an unvarnished view of 19th-century Parisian society. His ability to capture the essence of the moment and the soul of his subjects makes his work enduringly popular and influential to future generations of artists.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Museums and Exhibitions</strong><br>\nToulouse-Lautrec's art is widely celebrated and can be seen in museums around the world. Major retrospectives of his work provide insight into his creative genius and showcase his impact on the art world. The Musée Toulouse-Lautrec in Albi, France, which is dedicated to his art, holds the most extensive collection of his works.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Toulouse-Lautrec's Place in Modern Culture</strong><br>\nHenri de Toulouse-Lautrec's depiction of the Parisian underbelly has transcended time, influencing modern culture and art. His portrayal of the human condition in the city's nightlife, combined with his innovative poster art, continues to inspire modern graphic designers and artists. His life and work remain a subject of fascination and study, reflecting the enduring allure of his artistic vision.</p>\n"
              },
